      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠️ Cambridge 0580 Traps — Function Notation & Inverses",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Inverse vs. Reciprocal:</strong> \\(f^{-1}(x)\\) denotes the <em>inverse function</em>, NOT the reciprocal \\(\\frac{1}{f(x)}\\). For example, if \\(f(x) = 3x - 1\\), \\(f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x+1}{3}\\), NOT \\(\\frac{1}{3x-1}\\).</p><p><strong>Composite Function Order:</strong> The notation \\(fg(x)\\) means \\(f(g(x))\\) — evaluate \\(g(x)\\) first, then substitute that result into \\(f\\). Applying them in reverse order \\(gf(x)\\) yields a completely different function.</p><p><strong>Self-Inverse Functions:</strong> The graph of \\(f^{-1}(x)\\) is the reflection of \\(y = f(x)\\) in the line \\(y = x\\). Reciprocal functions \\(f(x) = \\frac{k}{x}\\) are self-inverse: \\(f^{-1}(x) = f(x)\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 9.C — Solving an equation with composite functions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Given \\(f(x) = 3x - 2\\) and \\(g(x) = \\frac{10}{x+1}\\), solve \\(fg(x) = 4\\).</p><p><strong>Step 1: Express \\(fg(x)\\):</strong></p>\\[fg(x) = f\\left(\\frac{10}{x+1}\\right) = 3\\left(\\frac{10}{x+1}\\right) - 2 = \\frac{30}{x+1} - 2\\]<p><strong>Step 2: Set equal to 4 and solve:</strong></p>\\[\\frac{30}{x+1} - 2 = 4 \\implies \\frac{30}{x+1} = 6 \\implies 30 = 6(x+1) \\implies x+1 = 5 \\implies x = 4\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "If \\(f(x) = 4x - 3\\) and \\(g(x) = x^2 + 1\\), find (a) \\(gf(2)\\) and (b) an expression for \\(g^{-1}(x)\\) for \\(x \\ge 1\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p><strong>(a) Inner function first:</strong> \\(f(2) = 4(2) - 3 = 5\\). Then \\(g(5) = 5^2 + 1 = 26\\).</p><p><strong>(b) Inverse of \\(g\\):</strong> Let \\(y = x^2 + 1 \\implies x^2 = y - 1 \\implies x = \\sqrt{y - 1}\\). Thus \\(g^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{x - 1}\\) for \\(x \\ge 1\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "[Functions · Practice 3] If the governing relation for this system is \\( g(t) = 5t + 5 \\), evaluate \\( g(8) \\) and find the inverse input \\( t \\) when \\( g(t) = 50 \\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Substitute \\( t = 8 \\): \\( g(8) = 5(8) + 5 = \\mathbf{45} \\). Setting \\( 5t + 5 = 50 \\) gives \\( 5t = 45 \\implies t = \\mathbf{9} \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "[Functions · Practice 4] If the governing relation for this system is \\( g(t) = 6t + 6 \\), evaluate \\( g(10) \\) and find the inverse input \\( t \\) when \\( g(t) = 72 \\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Substitute \\( t = 10 \\): \\( g(10) = 6(10) + 6 = \\mathbf{66} \\). Setting \\( 6t + 6 = 72 \\) gives \\( 6t = 66 \\implies t = \\mathbf{11} \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
